WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.067
This example uses CASE inside an aggregate.

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:08.837
For each admission, the CASE expression asks whether the diagnosis matches a condition.

00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:15.027
A matching row contributes a value such as one, and a non-matching row contributes zero.

00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:19.537
SUM then adds those contributions inside each patient group.

00:00:19.537 --> 00:00:23.847
Run the query and inspect the separate pneumonia and fracture counts.

00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:28.817
Before running it, choose one patient from the data and predict the two counts.

00:00:28.817 --> 00:00:31.687
Then compare your prediction with the result.

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:36.877
This pattern is flexible because CASE can handle more than simple equality.

00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:43.682
You can build conditions based on ranges, multiple columns, or more complex business rules.

00:00:43.682 --> 00:00:50.107
When using CASE for counting, make sure the values you return are appropriate for the aggregate you apply.

00:00:50.107 --> 00:00:55.072
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:55.072 --> 00:01:01.132
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:01:01.132 --> 00:01:03.612
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:03.612 --> 00:01:09.222
In a real setting such as a student-success report comparing course-level outcomes,

00:01:09.222 --> 00:01:15.437
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:15.437 --> 00:01:17.832
before discussing syntax.

00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:24.198
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
